{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/jolt/1794006000000-architect-j6g-ruling.gpt-6.1-sol.ruling.md collab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/jolt/1794006000000-architect-j6g-ruling.gpt-6.1-sol.ruling.md collab/jolt/1793990000000-architect-j6g-diagnose.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: filed by the orchestrator at the round's close\nCoding-Agent: codex gpt-6.1-sol (architect-review, read-only; session eb3d92db)\nSession-ID: eb3d92db-346b-43f6-a29d-77603374c3ca\n\n**J6g ruling — Session-ID: eb3d92db-346b-43f6-a29d-77603374c3ca**\n\n**Disposition:** J6g remains **FAILED**: 79 samples is below 600, and p99 ≈7.9 s exceeds 250 ms. Delivered-once and REPL advancement satisfy only their respective legs. The criterion stands. No code changes or remote runs were performed in this ruling round.\n\n**1. Scheduling principle**\n\n**A1’s transfer-count bounds do not bound an owner round’s synchronous interpretation or the elapsed interval between peer visits.** One read, one write, and correct descriptor rotation constrain operations per visit. They do not constrain compilation, expansion, indexing, publication, VM execution, message assembly, or callbacks executed between visits or within one visit.\n\nThe owner-thread contract shall state:\n\n> A composition claiming bounded service intervals must bound the synchronous work along every path between service opportunities. Work that can exceed that budget must return an explicit successor for later owner advancement. Transfer-count or instruction-count bounds alone establish no wall-time guarantee.\n\nChecking a deadline after an unbounded call returns does not enforce that deadline. Likewise, VM fuel cannot bound an unbounded host operation inside one instruction.\n\nThe source establishes a concrete violation of any history-independent interpretation bound: [yin.repl.index](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-orch/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:21) explicitly documents full-history publication and repeated watermark scanning, with quadratic total session cost. A finite five-iteration guest loop does not bound that surrounding work.\n\nDescriptor rotation is not ruled defective. The filed diagnosis establishes the growing source-level cost; it explicitly does **not** identify the historical hot instruction from remote stack evidence. The trace must supply that attribution.\n\n**2. Selected architecture and fix order**\n\n**Select (a), incremental index materialization, as the primary remedy for the confirmed history-growth mechanism. Select bounded advancement as the scheduling requirement wherever residual synchronous work exceeds the service budget. Do not select (c) without a measured assembly hotspot.**\n\nThe implementation order is evidence-driven:\n\n1. **Run the attribution trace before performance edits.** If code indexing dominates, proceed with (a). If another phase dominates, investigate that phase before treating index optimization as the sufficient J6g remedy.\n2. **Implement (a) when indexing attribution confirms it.** Then trace again to identify residual monopolization.\n3. **Apply (b) to the residual execution or publication work that requires yielding.** A VM-only change cannot repair synchronous publication before the VM runs.\n4. **Consider (c) only when pump timing and stack evidence identify payload processing near A’s final bytes.** That evidence may justify prioritizing this branch; it is not a mandatory third optimization.\n\nFor **(a)**, carry the publication cursor, covered roots, and allocator successor as explicit composition-owned derived state. Consume the committed suffix rather than reopening and reconstructing the entire history each evaluation. Retaining an allocator successor must preserve transactor authority and derive/validate it from authoritative history on recovery; the shell must not invent transaction times.\n\nThis ruling supersedes acceptance of repeated full-history rebuilding as the necessary price of every-evaluation publication. It **preserves that publication cadence**. Every successful publication must cover the committed frontier, materialize its required rows and index content, validate the manifest, and complete the applicable HEAD procedure before reporting publication success. Preserve atomic transactions, provenance, recovery, retry, and failure reporting. No history truncation, hidden global cache, canonical tuple field, or periodic-publication substitution is selected.\n\nIncrementality removes repeated reconstruction; it does **not** by itself prove a fixed service interval. Tree operations, validation, encoding, hashing, and storage work still require measurement and, where necessary, bounded advancement.\n\nFor **(b)**, retain pending execution or publication as an explicit successor advanced by the same owner. No worker thread, callback scheduler, or nested socket polling is selected.\n\nQuery visibility is ruled as follows:\n\n- While committed code remains unpublished, ordinary session queries return an explicit index-unavailable/pending refusal. They must not silently substitute the previous manifest.\n- Do not expose partially built indexes or combine published datoms with partially advanced AST relations.\n- Preserve publication-before-evaluation ordering for successful indexing. Publication failure continues through the existing explicit failure policy; yielding must not turn pending work into success.\n- Keep the previous verified manifest available for recovery and explicitly addressed snapshot use, without presenting it as the current session frontier.\n\nThis follows the existing [query snapshot contract](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-orch/src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:405).\n\nA yielded VM quantum is not evidence of a completed W evaluation. Preserve the registered submission and progress checks. If resumability requires changing what “every owner round” means in the harness, that requires a separate explicit workload ruling; it cannot silently certify this gate.\n\nFor **(c)**, a slow `:server-pump` phase alone is insufficient: it includes waiting, parsing, assembly, and callbacks. Require stack evidence in `take-payload`, `finish-frame`, `concat-chunks`, or their byte operations. A subsequent scoped change must preserve owned payloads, pure parser successors, message/control bounds, ordering, close behavior, and exactly-once complete-message delivery. Resumable assembly must withhold delivery until complete and expose runnable work through the existing deadline seam.\n\n**No adapter/parser change is selected on the present evidence. A1–A7 remain binding.**\n\n**3. Traced-run protocol and acceptance**\n\nOn the qualified remote checkout, record the commit, working diff, host, Jolt/native qualification, command, and configuration. Preserve stdout, stderr, and exit status.\n\nFirst verify instrumentation presence. This local checkout has the older first-round `W-PHASE` markers, but lacks the filed `JOLT_WORKLOAD_W_TRACE` implementation and nested phase/age reporting. An environment variable alone cannot establish that the remote checkout contains it.\n\nRun:\n\n```sh\nJOLT_WORKLOAD_W_TRACE=1 bb workload-w:jolt workload-w\n```\n\nThe trace must provide:\n\n- Flushed enter/leave records with round and monotonic timestamps for REPL work, A send, B send, server wait/pump, and client wait/pump; nested code-index and AST-index records.\n- Phase counts, total/max durations, retained transaction growth, owner rounds, verified REPL results, B sent/completed/outstanding counts, and A sent/delivered counts.\n- A’s first positive write and first delivery timestamps, with B samples restricted to requests sent and answered inside that interval.\n- Independent supervisor reporting of active nested phase, round, and age every ten seconds and at the unchanged 300,000 ms whole-run cap.\n\nParent timings include child timings; do not add them as independent costs. Obtain CPU stack samples during sustained slow phases to distinguish scanning/building from hashing, GC, VM work, assembly, callbacks, or native polling. Missing B submissions indicate owner/producer cadence failure, not automatically lost network replies.\n\n**The traced run is diagnostic and cannot PASS J6g.** Its intentional nonzero status must be distinguished from watchdog or worker failure.\n\nAfter the selected fix lands, repeat attribution and then run the unchanged, untraced gate:\n\n```sh\nbb workload-w:jolt workload-w\n```\n\nJ6g passes only when all registered conditions hold together:\n\n- A’s 4 MiB message is delivered exactly once.\n- At least **600** qualifying overlapping B round trips complete, with **p99 ≤250 ms** and no lost reply.\n- The required REPL evaluation advances every owner round, verified through actual shell/result progress.\n- The registered transfer pacing, duration, offered load, and outstanding-request limit are preserved.\n\nPost-transfer replies cannot fill the sample target. The 500-sample validity floor is not a pass threshold. A watchdog timeout, incomplete evidence, or any unmet leg leaves J6g unpassed. The five-minute cap is a safety limit, not a replacement transfer window.# J6g workload W diagnosis\n\n## Disposition\n\n**The relevant failure mechanism is synchronous work monopolizing the one\nowner, before or inside a pump turn. Descriptor rotation does not provide a\nwall-time bound on that work. There is a concrete, previously unaccounted-for\nsource of growing work: the harness submits a fresh program every round and\nthe REPL synchronously republishes the code index over its entire retained\nhistory before returning to either peer.** This is a composition scheduling\nproblem, exercised by this harness, rather than evidence of a broken\ndescriptor rotation.\n\nThere is an essential limit on the attribution: **the supplied observations do\nnot identify the executing instruction in either failed run.** Full-history\npublication is a confirmed source-level cost and the leading specific\nexplanation to measure, not a remotely observed stack sample. Neither “the\nindexer definitely spent 69 minutes in this call” nor “the parser definitely\nspun” follows from the banner, CPU utilization, and final sample total. This\nreport does not turn either into a fact. The original W brief and plan v3/v3.1\nare absent here; the diagnostic brief and the harness supply the criterion.\n\nNo adapter, parser, production owner, or other shared source was edited.\nThe two permitted harness files now provide the missing attribution evidence\nand fix several independently demonstrable measurement errors. The proposed\ncomposition change below is prose for an Architect ruling, not an implemented\nfix or a claim that W now passes. Per the brief's edits-only instruction, no\nworkload, build, or runtime test was executed locally.\n\n## The causal chain, with source locations\n\nLocations in the harness refer to the final files in this workspace; shared\nsource locations are unchanged.\n\n1. `src/dev/jolt_workload_w.clj:230` submits the same finite source loop anew\n   on **every** owner round. Its call at line 451 precedes A's send at line 474,\n   B's send at line 505, and both pumps at lines 531 and 536. This is a full\n   shell evaluation, not a VM instruction quantum. The loop's five iterations\n   do not bound compilation, expansion, indexing, publication, or collection.\n2. `src/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:593` reduces `handle-line` over input.\n   `handle-line:470` calls `evaluate-locally:172`, which calls\n   `repl/eval-input` synchronously at line 174. There is no intervening socket\n   poll or scheduler yield.\n3. `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:1649` increments the shell round and appends a new\n   program batch. Lines 1664-1666 run the code indexer and AST indexer before\n   the evaluator at line 1673. The code index stage at line 1180 calls\n   `yin.repl.index/step`.\n4. `src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:236` opens a new publication/transactor for\n   the round; creation at line 250 derives the next transaction time by\n   scanning the retained log. Each packet receives fresh occurrence ids and\n   provenance (`packet->tx-data:96`, `commit:287`), so repeating identical\n   source does not make this history disappear. `step:386` commits, writes\n   rows, calls `publish` at line 395, then drops the publication. `publish:346`\n   calls `transactor/publish!`, drains materialization, and reads back the\n   manifest before it returns.\n5. The cost is **explicitly documented** at\n   `src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:21`: publication rebuilds covered indexes over\n   the whole retained history, transactor opening rescans it, round cost\n   grows with session history, and total session cost grows quadratically.\n   `src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:117` is the watermark scan;\n   `src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:564` is the snapshot/build publication path.\n   This is actual code on W's path, not a generic speculation about slow VMs.\n6. The production composition has the same ordering boundary:\n   `src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc:1088` steps the shell;\n   `run-owner!:1302` completes `step-all` before `sleep!:1313`;\n   the jolt sleep policy at line 1437 finally polls/pumps. Production also\n   steps more interpreters than this manually assembled harness. This report\n   therefore identifies an applicable scheduling risk, not benchmark\n   equivalence between the two compositions.\n\nWhile a synchronous call holds the owner, A's producer cannot send, the\nserver cannot receive, B's producer cannot dispatch at 10 Hz, B's client\ncannot collect replies, and cooperative telemetry cannot print. CPU can be\nfully busy doing useful interpretation/publication work throughout. A round\nthat eventually returns can rotate descriptors correctly and still have\nmissed every useful service deadline. **The runtime summary does not\ncontradict the pump's rotation code. It contradicts the inference that a\nbounded number of visits implies a bounded service interval.**\n\nThere is a second such boundary *inside* the pump. At\n`src/jolt/dao/stream/ws/pump.jolt:659` a read synchronously enters parsing;\n`parse-input!:622` calls the parser and line 626 handles its event before the\nnext descriptor. `src/cljc/dao/stream/ws/rfc6455.cljc:337` slices/unmasks the\nincoming piece. At completion, `finish-frame:330` concatenates **the entire\nmessage**, using `concat-chunks:250`. Thus even a final 64 KiB read can trigger\na 4 MiB allocation/copy and delivery. `src/jolt/dao/host/bytes.jolt` implements\nslice/copy with element loops. This is real synchronous work; there is no\nevidence that this particular call, rather than the shell path, was hot in\nattempt 21. Importantly, assembly happens on message completion, not on every\nvisit to a partial message.\n\n## Disposition of the proposed causes\n\n| Candidate | Source evidence and conclusion |\n| --- | --- |\n| Broken rotation | `pump.jolt:809` rotates the ready handles after `last-id`; `pump!:834` visits each registered member of the snapshot once; `visit-connection!:678` reads once. This is count fairness. No demonstrated descriptor-selection defect; it does not establish time fairness. |\n| Repeated 4 MiB codec decode | Not this W path. `jolt.jolt:189` delivers binary payload directly to `:binary!`. A's callback at harness line 297 records its length; B's server callback echoes bytes. Negotiating CBOR does not install `ws/adapter`'s codec decoder here. The real transport decoder is in `src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc:400`, which these raw callbacks bypass. Final RFC message assembly remains a separate possible cost. |\n| A's write retry spins | Not the post-banner large-frame leg. `write-all!` at harness line 90 is used for the already-completed HTTP upgrade. `write-some!:131` does one `send-chunk!`; the main loop returns to the other work on `:would-block`. Its no-progress branch has no inner retry loop. The guard remains intact. |\n| Poll deadline never wakes relevant fds | `src/jolt/dao/stream/waitset/driver.jolt:176` collects interest, takes the minimum monotonic deadline, polls even when already due, then passes observed readiness to `pump!` at line 201. Each harness wait has a 5 ms cadence. No obvious deadline-only infinite loop explains CPU-bound work outside the wait. A native poll fault is not disproved by reading its caller; it needs a trace showing time spent in the pump/wait phase. |\n| Fresh shell evaluation and history publication | Confirmed synchronous, increasing work before both pumps, as traced above. Leading specific composition hypothesis; not yet a measured attribution of the historical hot instruction. |\n\nSeventy replies in 300 seconds also does **not** establish seventy lost\nrequests or a saturated eight-request window. The producer is on the same\nowner and dispatches at most once per returning round. It may have dispatched\nvery few requests at all. Likewise `p99 ABSENT` is the watchdog's deliberate\ninvalid-run report after a cap, not evidence of zero replies. The failed-run\nsummary lacks the sent/outstanding counters, per-phase timing, last active\ncall in later rounds, and stack samples required to distinguish these cases.\n\n## Harness corrections made\n\nOnly `src/dev/jolt_workload_w.clj` and\n`src/dev/jolt_workload_w_run.clj` were changed, in addition to this requested\nfindings file. Existing uncommitted edits in the workspace were preserved.\n\n- **Actual REPL work:** harness lines 450-458 now require the shell round to\n  advance and the driver to publish the expected result `5`. Previously the\n  harness incremented its own “step” counter after any returning driver call,\n  then compared two counters incremented in the same loop. That could accept\n  an error, an incomplete submission, or a non-evaluating driver call as\n  progress. This is evidence of a completed finite loop, not an invented VM\n  instruction counter.\n- **Send timestamps and cadence:** lines 498-507 take a fresh clock at\n  dispatch and just before send. Previously the timestamp preceded the entire\n  synchronous REPL evaluation and A send, so the alleged RTT included time\n  before submission to the socket seam. Dispatch uses absolute 100 ms slots,\n  starting with a request in the first round; missed slots are skipped, not\n  filled with bursts. A slow owner still fails to supply the required load;\n  this fix does not pretend that a same-owner producer is independent.\n- **Actual overlap:** `a-window` records the first positive write and first\n  delivery. Pacing starts with one frame byte and then spreads the remainder\n  across the configured duration from that positive write (lines 465-490).\n  Only B round trips whose send and reply fall within that interval emit\n  `W-SAMPLE` (lines 343-356). Previously replies after A's delivery could fill\n  the sample target. The owner now stops after A finishes and the duration\n  condition is met, rather than waiting for post-transfer replies to make the\n  count look sufficient (line 548). The 500 validity floor, 600 default target,\n  4 MiB payload, 250 ms threshold, and five-minute cap remain.\n- **Attribution:** `timed-call!:207` records call count, total time and maximum\n  time for the REPL, A send, B send, server pump/wait and client pump/wait.\n  `W-PROGRESS` records owner rounds, verified loop results, code transactions,\n  B sent/completed/outstanding counts, and A's window. Normal telemetry prints\n  these every ten seconds when the owner returns.\n- **A hung later round:** with `JOLT_WORKLOAD_W_TRACE=1`, every timed phase emits\n  a flushed enter/leave pair identifying its round. `step-repl!:230` also\n  wraps the public code-index and AST-index steps while that diagnostic\n  evaluation runs. These wrappers live only in the harness on its sole owner.\n  The supervisor's nested call stack (`jolt_workload_w_run.clj:34`) reports the\n  current phase, round and age independently every ten seconds and on cap.\n  The old first-round marker is retained under its explicitly historical\n  name. Parent timings include child timings; do not sum both as separate CPU\n  costs. Per-call output is deliberately diagnostic and a traced run cannot\n  PASS (`jolt_workload_w.clj:593`).\n\nThe prior five-minute independent watchdog and bounded `:would-block`\nhandling are preserved. These changes repair measurement and make attribution\npossible; they do not hide the expensive shell call by skipping evaluation,\nresetting its history, moving pumping before it, shrinking A, or weakening the\nregistered targets.\n\n## Proposed Architect ruling — no implementation in this round\n\n**Do not amend descriptor rotation or deadline folding on the evidence\navailable. Rule explicitly that A1's transfer-count bounds do not bound an\nowner round's synchronous interpretation, and require the next remote trace\nbefore selecting a performance implementation.**\n\nIf `:code-index` dominates, the concrete target is\n`yin.repl.index/open-publication:236`, `step:386`, and the\n`dao.space.transactor/publish!` → `dao.space.index/publish-index!` path:\n\n1. Replace repeated full-history construction/publication with an explicit\n   composition-owned incremental materialization of the appended transaction\n   suffix. Keep transaction-time allocation justified by the retained log;\n   recovery still derives it from authoritative history. Any in-process\n   watermark/index roots are derived state held by that composition, not\n   hidden globals or new persisted datom fields. Publish covered roots and\n   materialize/verify their referenced content before declaring the round\n   published, as today. The accepted per-evaluation publication cadence in\n   `yin.repl.index:21` is preserved, not silently changed to periodic publish.\n2. If bounded owner service also requires yielding within publication, make\n   the pending work an explicit successor value driven by the owner. That\n   requires a ruling about what queries observe while a publication is\n   pending. Do not silently make a query read an older coverage frontier or\n   bypass an indexer. The same applies to converting `repl/make-runner:689`\n   from run-to-completion to bounded VM execution: it is a composition\n   scheduling change, not a pump fix and not implied authorization to change\n   the workload from a fresh finite evaluation per round.\n\nIf instead `:server-pump` dominates near A's last bytes, target\n`rfc6455/take-payload:333`, `finish-frame:312`, and `concat-chunks:250`.\nPreserve the pure parser successor and owned payload semantics; improve byte\nslice/unmask/copy throughput or make assembly an explicit bounded resumable\nphase. In a resumable version retain offset/state, withhold the message event\nuntil fully assembled, report runnable work through the pump's existing\ndeadline mechanism, and deliver exactly once. Do not move codec execution\ninto the pump or deliver partial binary messages. This branch is conditional\non the trace, not a recommendation to optimize both paths speculatively.\n\nPreservation obligations for the ruling:\n\n| Existing obligation | Why this proposal can preserve it / required check |\n| --- | --- |\n| A1 owner and bounded visits | All successors are owned and advanced by the same owner; no worker callbacks, new threads, polling inside interpretation, or extra descriptor visits. Synchronous interpretation must be budgeted separately from visit count. |\n| A2 interest/deadline seam | Keep the existing `{:interest :deadline :pump!}` interface and monotonic clock; newly runnable held parser work makes the existing deadline due. Do not omit polling when a deadline is already due. |\n| A3 ordered output | No queue bypass, priority write, send-result change or byte-accounting change. Completion events/echo output retain per-connection order. |\n| A4 resource/control bounds | Preserve message-size checks before payload allocation, pong/close budgets, upgrade caps and deadlines. A resumable assembler retains at most the existing bounded message plus its explicit successor, not an unbounded side queue. |\n| A5 close/terminal behavior | Preserve first-close ownership, FIFO close placement, expiry, and exactly one terminal. Cancelling pending assembly cannot produce a later message callback after close. |\n| A6 lifecycle/isolation | Keep listener ownership, unbind grace, forced host cleanup and per-instance state; release pending derived work with its owning composition/connection. |\n| A7 | Its authoritative wording is not present in the supplied plan artifacts or source docstrings. No new wire protocol, portable transport outcome, callback contract or canonical tuple field is proposed; require the Architect to check the actual A7 text before approving. This report does not invent a seventh ruling to claim complete conformance. |\n\nThere is no demonstrated logical inconsistency in W itself. With sufficiently\nshort rounds and a transfer interval admitting 600 exchanges, its numbers are\ncompatible. This particular composition has no source-level time guarantee\nfor those rounds; a larger timeout cannot establish 10 Hz or 250 ms. The\nfresh-evaluation requirement and the synchronous all-history publication\npolicy may be incompatible with W on the measured host, but that is a\nmeasurable composition result, not a proof that the registered workload is\nuniversally unsatisfiable. “Within ~60 seconds” also lacks an exact upper\ntolerance in the available brief; a five-minute safety cap must not become\nthat tolerance.\n\n## Remote verification and decisive observations\n\nFirst run attribution on the orchestrator's qualified remote checkout:\n\n```sh\nJOLT_WORKLOAD_W_TRACE=1 bb workload-w:jolt workload-w\n```\n\nPreserve stdout, stderr and exit status. Nonzero status in trace mode is\nintentional. The watchdog still owns the 300,000 ms cap, including setup.\n\n- Increasing `:code-index` durations and transaction counts identify the\n  repeated-publication cost directly. A cap with active `:code-index` locates\n  the retained synchronous call there; use a CPU stack sample to distinguish\n  transactor scanning, building, encoding, hashing and GC inside it.\n- Active `:repl-step` without a nested active index step points to the rest of\n  frontend/expansion/evaluation or its surrounding work, not the socket pump.\n- Active `:server-pump` near the final send calls for a stack sample in\n  parsing/assembly/callbacks versus native poll. That phase intentionally\n  includes both wait and pump; its name alone cannot distinguish them.\n- Many short calls with `b-sent` low identify producer/owner cadence failure;\n  `b-sent` increasing with eight outstanding identifies a different transport\n  or response-collection failure. A send stuck at `:would-block` still yields\n  each round, leaving the other phase records observable.\n\nAfter attribution and any separately approved composition fix, run the\nuntraced criterion:\n\n```sh\nbb workload-w:jolt workload-w\n```\n\nRequire the original targets and the actual transfer interval, rather than\naccepting a PASS label alone. The same-owner load generator remains a\nlimitation: if the intended experiment requires an externally sustained\n10 Hz offered load even while the server owner is busy, A/B need independent\nproducer owners/processes in a separate harness change. The new sent counters\nmake that limitation visible instead of calling missing submissions lost\nnetwork replies.\n\n## Checks performed\n\n- `cljstyle fix` followed by `cljstyle check` on the two permitted Clojure\n  files: exit 0.\n- `git diff --check -- src/dev/jolt_workload_w.clj src/dev/jolt_workload_w_run.clj`:\n  exit 0.\n- No workload, runtime tests, build, remote command, adapter edit, commit or\n  staging operation. The checkout has no local `build/tools/jolt-env.sh`;\n  no other worktree's build artifacts were reused. Runtime behavior of the\n  new tracing and measurement guards remains for remote verification.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
